</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Inner Product for Wave Functions</title>
             
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       The inner product for wave functions is similar to what we've introduced earlier for general functions,
                       but we need to take more care for spaces over <m>\C</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    For two complex-valued functions <m>f</m> and <m>g</m>, we define 
                    <md>
                        \langle f, g \rangle := \int \overline{f} \cdot g \quad dx
                    </md>
                    This satisfies the necessary properties of an inner product. In particular, <m>\langle f, f\rangle \geq 0</m>, 
                    which is the reason we need the conjugate of one of the functions in the definition.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Note that computing <m>\langle f, f\rangle</m> is necessary for normalizing the square of a wave function, which
                    is used as a probability distribution:
                    <md>
                        \langle f, f\rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{f} \cdot f \quad dx = \int_{-\infty}^\infty |f|^2 \quad dx
                    </md>
                    As long as the last integral converges, we can multiply by the necessary constant to normalize.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Completeness</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
         Convergent series of wave functions are again valid wave functions.
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                If <m>\{\psi_n\}_{n=1}^\infty</m> is a set of valid wave functions and <m>\psi = \sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n\psi_n</m>
                converges, then <m>\psi</m> is a valid wave function.
            </p>
            <p>
                In contrast, there are examples of vector spaces which are not complete:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\Q</m> (over <m>\Q</m>). We can sum infinitely many rational numbers to converge to <m>\pi</m> (an irrational number).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The set of all polynomials on the interval <m>[0,1]</m>. Recall that we built Taylor series
                            to converge to non-polynomial functions. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Summary</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
          When working with a set of valid wave functions, we can
        </p>
       </statement> 
       <solution>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        determine whether two functions are orthogonal (independent) using the inner product
                        <m>\langle f, g \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{f} \cdot g \quad dx</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        form convergent series of wave functions and know the series is also a valid wave function.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
       </solution>
       
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
